# Checksum lists are machine-read, byte-exact files, so git must never rewrite
# their line endings.
#
# With the default `core.autocrlf=true` on Windows, a checkout turns the LF
# blob into CRLF on disk. `sha256sum -c` then parses the recorded *file name*
# as "nyrqis-live.iso\r", cannot open it, and reports
#
#     sha256sum: 'nyrqis-live.iso'$'\r': No such file or directory
#
# so `tools/live_iso_selfcheck.sh` -- the negative control that proves the
# gate goes red on a broken disc -- fails on its own setup rather than on the
# defect it is meant to inject. The digest is also read straight out of these
# files by `tools/check_live_iso.sh`, so the bytes on disk have to be the
# bytes the build wrote.
#
# `-text` disables conversion in both directions: the blob stays LF, the
# worktree stays LF, and the digest a gate reads is the digest it recorded.
*.sha256 -text

# This file is a rule list, not prose: it has no reason to be platform-specific
# either, and converting it would make these comments render with stray
# carriage returns in every Windows editor that shows the file.
.gitattributes -text
